  "band_reachability_power": {
    "512MiB": {
      "n_slot_means": 20,
      "slot_mean_span_pct": 18.28,
      "band_width_pct": 0.2047,
      "p_single_slot_in_band_uniform_model": 0.0112,
      "expected_slot_means_in_band": 0.224,
      "p_at_least_one_in_band_pct": 20.2,
      "band_width_vs_single_draw_noise": {
        "median_round_spread_pct": 0.597,
        "band_width_pct": 0.2047,
        "ratio_noise_to_band": 2.9,
        "band_resolvable_by_one_draw": false
      },
      "conclusion": "with 20 slot means spanning 18.28% and a band 0.2047% wide, a uniform model expects 0.22 of them in band and gives only a 20% chance that any lands there. Zero in-band is therefore not evidence against the historical value -- this protocol has almost no power in that direction. The defensible statement is symmetric: at this size and sample size the data neither authenticate nor exclude a value in the band. A second limit, visible only once the timing rounds were retained: a single draw's own round-to-round spread has median 0.597%, which is 2.9x the band width. Even with slot, process and program all held fixed, one measurement cannot resolve an interval this narrow. The placement argument was never the binding constraint."
